-- Q1: medium / L3 — Section 453(e) related party resale triggers deferred gain
(6, 'Nontaxable Dispositions', $EXPL$Hayward sells farmland (basis $100,000) to her daughter for $400,000 on a 10-year installment note in Year 1. In Year 2, the daughter resells the farmland to an unrelated buyer for $420,000. At the time of the daughter's resale, Hayward has received only $40,000 in payments. Under Section 453(e), what amount of gain must Hayward recognize in Year 2 as a result of the daughter's resale?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$0 because Hayward's installment payments have not changed", "$300,000 — all remaining deferred gain is triggered immediately", "$270,000 — gain on the deemed payment of $360,000 at the 75% gross profit ratio", "$120,000 — gain limited to the daughter's resale proceeds minus Hayward's basis"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 453(e), when a related-party buyer resells within 2 years, the amount realized by the related party is treated as a deemed payment to the original seller. The daughter's resale triggers a deemed payment equal to the lesser of the resale price ($420,000) or the original installment obligation balance ($360,000 remaining). Hayward's gross profit percentage is ($400,000 − $100,000) / $400,000 = 75%. Deemed payment gain = $360,000 × 75% = $270,000. Wrong (A): Section 453(e) specifically overrides normal installment timing when the related party disposes of the property within 2 years. Wrong (B): $300,000 is total original gain, but Hayward already recognized $30,000 on the $40,000 received; the deemed payment is capped at the remaining obligation. Wrong (D): $120,000 uses an incorrect formula unrelated to the gross profit percentage method.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Section 453A(d) pledge of installment obligation treated as payment
(6, 'Nontaxable Dispositions', $EXPL$Vasquez sold commercial real estate on the installment method (selling price $6,000,000, basis $2,000,000, gross profit percentage 66.67%). The outstanding installment obligation is $4,500,000. Vasquez pledges this obligation as security for a $3,000,000 bank loan. Under Section 453A(d), how is the pledge treated for tax purposes?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The pledge has no tax effect until Vasquez defaults on the loan", "The net proceeds of the secured debt ($3,000,000) are treated as a payment received on the installment obligation", "Only interest is imputed on the pledged amount at the applicable federal rate", "The entire obligation is treated as disposed of under Section 453B"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Section 453A(d) provides that if an installment obligation arising from a sale exceeding $5,000,000 is pledged as security for any indebtedness, the net proceeds of the secured debt are treated as a payment on the installment obligation. Vasquez must recognize $3,000,000 × 66.67% = $2,000,100 in gain. Wrong (A): Section 453A(d) creates an immediate deemed payment event upon pledge, not upon default. Wrong (C): The pledge rule triggers gain recognition, not merely an interest charge (which is a separate Section 453A(a) provision). Wrong (D): A pledge is not a disposition under Section 453B; it creates a deemed payment under Section 453A(d).$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: easy / L1 — Section 453(b)(2) exclusion for dealer dispositions
(6, 'Nontaxable Dispositions', $EXPL$Which type of property sale is specifically excluded from the installment method under Section 453(b)(2)?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Sale of a rental property held for investment for 10 years", "Sale of unimproved land held for investment", "Sale of personal property by a dealer who regularly sells such property on the installment plan", "Sale of a vacation home used for personal purposes"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 453(b)(2) excludes dealer dispositions — sales of personal property by a person who regularly sells or otherwise disposes of personal property of the same type on the installment plan. Such dealers must recognize gain in full in the year of sale. Wrong (A): Rental property held for investment qualifies for the installment method because the seller is not a dealer. Wrong (B): Unimproved investment land is eligible for installment sale reporting. Wrong (D): A vacation home is personal-use property, not dealer inventory, and can use the installment method.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q4: medium / L3 — Section 453(i) recapture income recognized in year of sale
(6, 'Nontaxable Dispositions', $EXPL$Thornton sells manufacturing equipment for $500,000 on a 5-year installment plan ($100,000/year). The equipment has an adjusted basis of $150,000 and original cost of $400,000, with $250,000 of Section 1245 depreciation recapture potential. Under Section 453(i), how much total income must Thornton recognize in Year 1?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$70,000 — proportional share of total gain applied to the Year 1 payment", "$250,000 — Section 1245 recapture only, with no additional installment gain", "$270,000 — the $250,000 recapture plus $20,000 installment gain on the $100,000 payment", "$350,000 — total gain recognized immediately"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 453(i), all Section 1245 ordinary income recapture ($250,000) must be recognized in the year of sale regardless of payments received. Total gain = $500,000 − $150,000 = $350,000. Remaining capital gain after recapture = $350,000 − $250,000 = $100,000, spread over installments. For installment computation, basis is increased by the recapture amount: $150,000 + $250,000 = $400,000. Remaining GP% = ($500,000 − $400,000) / $500,000 = 20%. Year 1 installment capital gain = $100,000 × 20% = $20,000. Total Year 1 income = $250,000 + $20,000 = $270,000. Wrong (A): $70,000 uses the unadjusted 70% GP ratio and ignores mandatory recapture acceleration. Wrong (B): $250,000 omits the installment gain component recognized on the Year 1 payment. Wrong (D): $350,000 treats all gain as recognized immediately, but the $100,000 capital gain portion is spread over the installment period.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q5: easy / L2 — Section 1033(h) principal residence in disaster area
(6, 'Nontaxable Dispositions', $EXPL$Torres's principal residence is destroyed by a federally declared disaster. Torres receives $350,000 in insurance proceeds (adjusted basis was $180,000). Under Section 1033(h), what special rule applies to gains from involuntary conversions of principal residences in disaster areas?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No gain may be deferred; all insurance proceeds are taxable", "The Section 121 exclusion is applied first before determining the amount eligible for Section 1033 deferral", "The replacement period is reduced to 1 year for disaster-area residences", "The taxpayer must purchase an identical home in the same neighborhood"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Section 1033(h)(1) coordinates with Section 121 for principal residences destroyed in federally declared disasters. The Section 121 exclusion ($250,000 single / $500,000 MFJ) applies first to reduce or eliminate the gain, and any remaining gain can then be deferred under Section 1033 by purchasing qualified replacement property within the replacement period. Wrong (A): Both the Section 121 exclusion and Section 1033 deferral are available, potentially eliminating all tax. Wrong (C): The replacement period is actually extended to 4 years for principal residences in federally declared disaster areas. Wrong (D): The replacement property must be similar in use but does not need to be identical or in the same neighborhood.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q6: hard / L4 — Evaluate Section 453(e) exception for involuntary disposition by related party
(6, 'Nontaxable Dispositions', $EXPL$Lambert sells timber acreage (basis $200,000) to his son for $800,000 on a 10-year installment note in 2025. In 2026, the county condemns the son's property for a reservoir project, paying the son $850,000. Lambert's CPA must determine whether Section 453(e) acceleration applies. Assess the correct tax treatment.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Section 453(e) applies — the condemnation is a second disposition triggering gain acceleration for Lambert", "Section 453(e) applies — but gain is limited to the son's actual profit on the resale ($50,000)", "Section 453(e) does not apply — involuntary conversions are specifically excepted from the related party resale acceleration rule", "Section 453(e) does not apply — Lambert must amend his prior return to report the full gain in the year of the original sale"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 453(e)(6)(A) provides a specific exception to the related party resale acceleration rule for any second disposition that is an involuntary conversion (under Section 1033) if the first disposition occurred before the threat of conversion. Since the county condemnation is an involuntary conversion and Lambert's original sale preceded the condemnation threat, the exception applies. Lambert continues reporting under the installment method without acceleration. Wrong (A): While condemnation is a disposition, Section 453(e)(6)(A) specifically excepts involuntary conversions from triggering acceleration. Wrong (B): This attempts to limit gain but the entire premise is inapplicable; the involuntary conversion exception removes the provision entirely. Wrong (D): No amended return is required; Lambert's original installment election remains valid.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Section 453(k) exclusion for publicly traded stock
(6, 'Nontaxable Dispositions', $EXPL$Crawford sells 10,000 shares of a publicly traded corporation to a private buyer for $500,000, payable in 5 annual installments of $100,000 each. Crawford's basis in the shares is $200,000. Can Crawford use the installment method to defer the $300,000 gain?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Yes, because the buyer is not a related party", "Yes, because the total sale exceeds $150,000", "No, because Section 453(k)(2) excludes installment sales of stock or securities traded on an established securities market", "No, because stock sales must always be reported in full on Schedule D"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 453(k)(2) specifically excludes any sale of stock or securities that are traded on an established securities market from installment method treatment, regardless of who the buyer is. Crawford must recognize the entire $300,000 gain in the year of sale. Wrong (A): The identity of the buyer is irrelevant; the exclusion is based on the character of the property as publicly traded. Wrong (B): There is no $150,000 threshold that unlocks installment method treatment for traded securities. Wrong (D): While stock sales are reported on Schedule D, the reason installment treatment is unavailable here is the specific Section 453(k)(2) exclusion, not a general Schedule D rule.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — Section 1033 replacement period for general involuntary conversions
(6, 'Nontaxable Dispositions', $EXPL$What is the standard replacement period for an involuntary conversion of personal property (not real property) under Section 1033(a)(2)(B)?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["1 year from the date of the conversion event", "2 years after the close of the first tax year in which any part of the gain is realized", "3 years after the close of the first tax year in which any part of the gain is realized", "5 years from the date insurance proceeds are received"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Section 1033(a)(2)(B) provides a 2-year replacement period measured from the close of the first tax year in which any part of the gain upon conversion is realized. This is the standard period for personal property and non-condemned real property. Wrong (A): The period is 2 years from the close of the tax year, not 1 year from the event. Wrong (C): The 3-year period applies only to condemned real property held for productive use or investment under Section 1033(g). Wrong (D): The period runs from the close of the tax year of gain realization, not from receipt of insurance proceeds, and is 2 years, not 5.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: medium / L3 — Section 453B(a) sale of installment obligation to third party
(6, 'Nontaxable Dispositions', $EXPL$Petrov originally sold land on the installment method (selling price $600,000, basis $240,000). After collecting $200,000 in payments, Petrov sells the remaining installment obligation (face value $400,000) to an unrelated investor for $370,000. Petrov's basis in the remaining obligation is $160,000. Under Section 453B(a), what gain must Petrov recognize on the sale of the obligation?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$240,000 — the total deferred gain on the remaining obligation", "$160,000 — the original basis allocated to the remaining payments", "$210,000 — the $370,000 sale price minus the $160,000 basis in the obligation", "$400,000 — the full face value of the obligation"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 453B(a), when an installment obligation is sold, gain equals the amount realized ($370,000) minus the basis in the obligation ($160,000) = $210,000. The basis in the remaining obligation is the original $240,000 basis minus basis already recovered ($80,000 on the $200,000 collected at 40% basis ratio) = $160,000. Wrong (A): $240,000 is the total deferred gain remaining in the obligation, not the gain on the discounted sale to the investor. Wrong (B): $160,000 is the basis in the obligation, not the gain. Wrong (D): $400,000 is the face value, not the amount realized or the taxable gain.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q10: hard / L4 — Evaluate whether Section 453(f)(6) boot in a 1031 exchange qualifies for installment
(6, 'Nontaxable Dispositions', $EXPL$Nakamura exchanges a commercial building (FMV $900,000, adjusted basis $350,000) for a like-kind office building (FMV $700,000) plus a $200,000 installment note payable over 4 years. Nakamura's CPA must determine how to report the $200,000 of boot received as an installment note. Assess the correct treatment.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The $200,000 boot is taxable immediately in full; the installment method cannot be used for boot from a like-kind exchange", "The $200,000 note is reported under the installment method, with gain recognized as payments are received, up to the $550,000 total realized gain", "The $200,000 note reduces the basis of the like-kind property received but triggers no gain", "The entire $550,000 gain is recognized because the exchange fails the like-kind requirements"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Section 453(f)(6)(C) allows the installment method for boot received in the form of an installment obligation in a Section 1031 like-kind exchange. The total realized gain is $900,000 − $350,000 = $550,000, but recognized gain is limited to the boot received ($200,000). As installment payments are received, gain is recognized proportionally. The gross profit ratio on the boot portion is $200,000/$200,000 = 100%, so each payment is fully taxable until the $200,000 is reported. Wrong (A): Section 453(f)(6)(C) specifically permits installment reporting of boot from like-kind exchanges. Wrong (C): Boot in excess of basis deferral triggers gain; it does not simply reduce basis with no recognition. Wrong (D): The exchange qualifies for like-kind treatment on the real property portion; only the boot is potentially taxable.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q11: easy / L2 — Section 453(d) election out is irrevocable
(6, 'Nontaxable Dispositions', $EXPL$Palmer sells investment land for $400,000 (basis $150,000) on a 5-year installment note. Palmer elects out of the installment method on a timely filed return, reporting the full $250,000 gain in Year 1. In Year 2, Palmer wants to revoke the election and switch to installment reporting. Can Palmer revoke the election?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Yes, by filing an amended return for Year 1 within 3 years", "Yes, by simply reporting installment income beginning in Year 2", "No, the election out under Section 453(d) is generally irrevocable without IRS consent", "Yes, by filing Form 6252 for Year 2 and all subsequent years"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 453(d), the election to report a sale without using the installment method is irrevocable without the consent of the IRS. Once the taxpayer elects out and reports the full gain, the election generally cannot be undone by filing an amended return. Wrong (A): Filing an amended return does not automatically revoke a Section 453(d) election; IRS consent is required. Wrong (B): The taxpayer cannot unilaterally switch to installment reporting in subsequent years after electing out. Wrong (D): Form 6252 cannot be filed for subsequent years if the taxpayer elected out; the full gain was already reported.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Section 1033 net condemnation proceeds after expenses
(6, 'Nontaxable Dispositions', $EXPL$Reeves's office building (adjusted basis $500,000) is condemned by the state. The condemnation award is $800,000, but Reeves incurs $50,000 in legal fees and $30,000 in appraisal costs to contest the initial award amount. Under Section 1033, what amount constitutes the "amount realized" for determining gain and the reinvestment threshold?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$800,000 — expenses do not reduce the amount realized in a condemnation", "$720,000 — the award reduced by both legal and appraisal costs", "$750,000 — only legal fees reduce the award, not appraisal costs", "$880,000 — expenses are added to the award"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Expenses incurred to obtain or contest a condemnation award (legal fees, appraisal costs, expert witness fees) reduce the amount realized. Net condemnation proceeds = $800,000 − $50,000 − $30,000 = $720,000. Gain = $720,000 − $500,000 = $220,000. The reinvestment threshold is $720,000 (net proceeds). Wrong (A): Condemnation expenses directly connected to obtaining the award reduce the amount realized, similar to selling expenses. Wrong (C): Both legal fees and appraisal costs incurred to contest or secure the award are deducted from the amount realized. Wrong (D): Expenses reduce rather than increase the amount realized.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Section 453(j)(2) selling price adjustment and basis reallocation
(6, 'Nontaxable Dispositions', $EXPL$Burke sold equipment on a 5-year installment plan (original selling price $500,000, basis $200,000, gross profit percentage 60%). After receiving $300,000 in payments (recognizing $180,000 gain), a price adjustment reduces the total selling price to $400,000. The remaining payments total $100,000. What is Burke's adjusted gross profit percentage for the remaining payments?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["60% — the original gross profit percentage continues unchanged", "20% — recomputed as ($400,000 − $200,000 − $180,000) / $100,000", "40% — recomputed as ($400,000 − $200,000) / $500,000", "100% — the remaining payments are all gain since basis was fully recovered"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 453(j)(2) and Treasury Regulations, when the selling price is retroactively reduced, the gross profit percentage is recomputed for remaining payments. New total gain = $400,000 − $200,000 = $200,000. Gain already recognized = $180,000. Remaining gain = $20,000. Remaining payments = $100,000. New GP% = $20,000 / $100,000 = 20%. Wrong (A): The original 60% cannot continue when the selling price has been reduced; the remaining gain is much smaller. Wrong (C): 40% uses the revised total gain divided by the original selling price, which is not the correct recomputation method. Wrong (D): Basis has not been fully recovered; $20,000 of the $200,000 gain remains to be recognized.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — Section 1033 condemnation of inventory does not qualify
(6, 'Nontaxable Dispositions', $EXPL$A developer holds 50 lots as inventory for sale to customers. The city condemns 10 lots for a park, paying the developer $600,000 (basis $400,000 in the condemned lots). Can the developer defer the $200,000 gain under Section 1033?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Yes, any condemned property qualifies for Section 1033 deferral regardless of its character", "Yes, but only if the developer replaces with identical lots in the same subdivision", "No, dealer inventory does not qualify for meaningful Section 1033 deferral", "No, because the gain is below the $500,000 statutory minimum for condemnation deferral"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): While Section 1033 applies broadly to involuntary conversions, the practical benefit of deferral is limited for inventory property because gain on property held primarily for sale is ordinary income and the replacement property rules are restrictive. Under Section 1033(a), gain from condemned inventory is treated as ordinary income; the taxpayer cannot use Section 1033(g)'s like-kind standard, and the similar-use test is extremely narrow for dealer property. Wrong (A): Not all condemned property benefits equally from Section 1033; inventory has significant limitations. Wrong (B): Even with identical replacement, dealer property faces character and recognition constraints. Wrong (D): There is no $500,000 threshold for Section 1033 eligibility.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: hard / L4 — Evaluate installment sale combined with Section 1239 recharacterization
(6, 'Nontaxable Dispositions', $EXPL$Caldwell sells a patent (adjusted basis $50,000, held over 1 year) to a corporation she controls (90% ownership) for $250,000 on a 5-year installment plan ($50,000/year). Caldwell's CPA must determine the character of the gain. Under Section 1239 and Section 453, assess the correct treatment of the installment payments.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Each payment produces $40,000 of long-term capital gain (80% gross profit ratio)", "Each payment produces $40,000 of ordinary income because Section 1239 recharacterizes the entire gain as ordinary income due to the related-party sale of depreciable property", "Each payment produces $40,000 of Section 1231 gain because the patent is a business asset", "Installment method is unavailable; all $200,000 of gain is recognized in Year 1 as capital gain"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Section 1239 recharacterizes gain on the sale of depreciable property (or amortizable property such as a patent) to a related party (controlled entity) as ordinary income rather than capital gain. The installment method remains available under Section 453, but each payment carries the ordinary income character imposed by Section 1239. Gross profit = $200,000 / $250,000 = 80%. Each $50,000 payment produces $40,000 of ordinary income. Wrong (A): Section 1239 converts what would otherwise be capital gain into ordinary income; the gain cannot retain capital gain character. Wrong (C): Section 1231 treatment is overridden by the specific recharacterization rule in Section 1239 for related-party sales. Wrong (D): The installment method is not disallowed by Section 1239; only Section 453(g) disallows installment treatment for depreciable property sales to related parties, and that applies to personal property only if Section 1239(b) related.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Section 1033 basis of replacement property when fully reinvested
(6, 'Nontaxable Dispositions', $EXPL$Sutton's restaurant building (adjusted basis $350,000) is destroyed by fire. Insurance pays $550,000. Sutton purchases a new restaurant building for $600,000 within the replacement period and elects Section 1033 deferral. What is Sutton's basis in the new building?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$600,000 — the cost of the new building", "$550,000 — the insurance proceeds received", "$400,000 — the cost of the new building minus the deferred gain", "$350,000 — the original basis of the destroyed building"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): When the taxpayer fully reinvests (replacement cost exceeds proceeds), all gain is deferred. Realized gain = $550,000 − $350,000 = $200,000. Since Sutton reinvested $600,000 (more than the $550,000 proceeds), the entire $200,000 gain is deferred. Basis of replacement = cost minus deferred gain = $600,000 − $200,000 = $400,000. Wrong (A): The cost basis is reduced by the deferred gain to preserve the built-in tax liability. Wrong (B): The insurance proceeds are not the basis; replacement cost minus deferred gain determines basis. Wrong (D): The original basis ($350,000) would apply only if replacement cost exactly equaled insurance proceeds.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: medium / L3 — Section 453(e)(2) death exception to related party acceleration
(6, 'Nontaxable Dispositions', $EXPL$In 2025, Wagner sells a rental property to his brother for $500,000 on a 10-year installment note (basis $200,000). In 2026, Wagner dies and his estate inherits the installment obligation. The brother resells the property to an unrelated buyer for $520,000 in 2027. Under Section 453(e), does the brother's resale trigger acceleration of the remaining deferred gain for Wagner's estate?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Yes, the resale within 2 years triggers full acceleration regardless of the seller's death", "Yes, but only to the extent the resale price exceeds the original installment price", "No, Section 453(e)(6)(A) provides that the related party resale rule does not apply when the second disposition occurs after the death of the original seller", "No, because installment obligations inherited by an estate are automatically converted to cash basis"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 453(e)(6)(A) provides an exception to the related party resale acceleration rule when the second disposition occurs after the death of the person who made the first disposition. Wagner (original seller) died in 2026, and the brother's resale occurred in 2027, after Wagner's death. Therefore, the acceleration rule does not apply, and Wagner's estate continues installment reporting without acceleration. Wrong (A): Section 453(e)(6)(A) specifically excepts situations where the original seller dies before the related party's resale. Wrong (B): This misstates the rule; the death exception is complete, not limited to excess resale proceeds. Wrong (D): Estates continue installment reporting under IRD rules; there is no automatic conversion to cash basis.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q18: easy / L2 — Section 1033 recognized gain when not fully reinvested
(6, 'Nontaxable Dispositions', $EXPL$Ortega's business equipment (adjusted basis $60,000) is stolen. Insurance pays $100,000. Ortega purchases replacement equipment for $85,000 within the replacement period and elects Section 1033 deferral. How much gain must Ortega recognize?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$40,000 — the full realized gain", "$0 — all gain is deferred regardless of reinvestment amount", "$15,000 — the amount of insurance proceeds not reinvested in replacement property", "$25,000 — the difference between the replacement cost and the original basis"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 1033(a)(2), recognized gain equals the lesser of (1) realized gain ($100,000 − $60,000 = $40,000) or (2) proceeds not reinvested ($100,000 − $85,000 = $15,000). Since $15,000 < $40,000, Ortega recognizes $15,000. Wrong (A): The full $40,000 gain is not recognized because Ortega reinvested most of the proceeds. Wrong (B): Gain is deferred only to the extent of reinvestment; the $15,000 shortfall is taxable. Wrong (D): $25,000 incorrectly computes gain as replacement cost minus original basis.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q19: hard / L4 — Evaluate charitable donation of installment obligation
(6, 'Nontaxable Dispositions', $EXPL$Ingram sold land on the installment method (selling price $400,000, basis $160,000). After collecting $100,000 in payments, Ingram donates the remaining installment obligation (face value $300,000, FMV $280,000, basis in obligation $120,000) to a qualified public charity. Ingram's CPA must determine both the gain triggered and the charitable deduction available. Assess the correct treatment.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Gain of $160,000 under Section 453B; charitable deduction of $280,000 (FMV)", "Gain of $160,000 under Section 453B; charitable deduction of $120,000 (basis only, because gain property donated)", "No gain — the donation is a nontaxable transfer; charitable deduction of $280,000", "Gain of $160,000 under Section 453B; charitable deduction of $280,000 reduced by the $160,000 ordinary income component"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 453B, any disposition of an installment obligation — including a charitable gift — triggers gain equal to the FMV of the obligation minus the basis: $280,000 − $120,000 = $160,000. Separately, the charitable contribution deduction is based on the FMV of the obligation ($280,000) as donated property. The gain recognition and deduction are computed independently. Wrong (B): For long-term capital gain property donated to a public charity, the deduction is generally at FMV, not limited to basis. Wrong (C): Section 453B treats all dispositions, including gifts, as triggering the deferred gain. Wrong (D): The charitable deduction for long-term capital gain property donated to a public charity is the full FMV without reduction for the gain component.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — Definition of "amount realized" in involuntary conversion
(6, 'Nontaxable Dispositions', $EXPL$In the context of an involuntary conversion under Section 1033, what does "amount realized" represent?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The adjusted basis of the destroyed or condemned property", "The cost of the replacement property purchased by the taxpayer", "The insurance proceeds, condemnation award, or other compensation received for the converted property", "The fair market value of the converted property on the date of acquisition"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): In an involuntary conversion, the amount realized is the total compensation received — whether insurance proceeds, condemnation award, or other recovery — for the converted property. Gain is computed as amount realized minus the adjusted basis of the converted property. Wrong (A): Adjusted basis is subtracted from the amount realized to determine gain; it is not the amount realized itself. Wrong (B): The cost of replacement property determines how much gain is deferred, not the amount realized. Wrong (D): Fair market value at acquisition is relevant to original basis, not to the amount realized upon conversion.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: medium / L2 — Section 453 return of basis ordering within each payment
(6, 'Nontaxable Dispositions', $EXPL$Chang sells raw land for $200,000 on a 4-year installment plan ($50,000/year) with an adjusted basis of $80,000. No mortgage is assumed. Chang's gross profit percentage is 60%. In Year 3, Chang receives the $50,000 installment payment. How is this payment allocated between gain and return of basis?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$50,000 gain and $0 return of basis", "$30,000 gain and $20,000 return of basis", "$20,000 gain and $30,000 return of basis", "$0 gain and $50,000 return of basis (basis recovered first)"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 453, each installment payment is allocated between gain and return of basis using the gross profit percentage. GP% = ($200,000 − $80,000) / $200,000 = 60%. Each $50,000 payment: gain = $50,000 × 60% = $30,000; return of basis = $50,000 × 40% = $20,000. This allocation is the same for every payment year. Wrong (A): Not all of each payment is gain; 40% represents a nontaxable return of basis. Wrong (C): This reverses the gain and basis portions. Wrong (D): The installment method does not use a basis-first recovery; gain and basis are allocated proportionally in each payment.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q22: medium / L3 — Section 1033 condemnation of depreciable property — recapture implications
(6, 'Nontaxable Dispositions', $EXPL$Spencer's manufacturing equipment (original cost $300,000, accumulated depreciation $180,000, adjusted basis $120,000) is condemned by the state for $250,000. Spencer purchases replacement equipment for $260,000 within the replacement period. Under Section 1033, how much of the $130,000 realized gain is recognized, and what is the character of any recognized gain?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$0 recognized — all gain is deferred because Spencer fully reinvested", "$130,000 of ordinary income under Section 1245 — recapture cannot be deferred", "$0 recognized — Section 1033 defers all gain including Section 1245 recapture when proceeds are fully reinvested", "$180,000 of ordinary income — all depreciation is recaptured regardless of reinvestment"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): When a taxpayer fully reinvests involuntary conversion proceeds in qualified replacement property, Section 1033 defers the entire gain, including any Section 1245 depreciation recapture component. Unlike installment sales (where Section 453(i) accelerates recapture), Section 1033 deferral overrides the recapture provisions when full reinvestment occurs. Wrong (A): While the result ($0 recognized) is correct, choice A does not address the critical recapture issue. Wrong (B): Section 1245 recapture is deferred under Section 1033 when the taxpayer fully reinvests; this is a key distinction from installment sale rules. Wrong (D): $180,000 is total depreciation, but no recapture is triggered when all proceeds are reinvested under Section 1033.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q23: easy / L2 — Section 453 interest income separate from gain
(6, 'Nontaxable Dispositions', $EXPL$Dunn sells investment property on a 5-year installment note. Each annual payment of $100,000 includes $8,000 of stated interest at the applicable federal rate. Dunn's gross profit percentage is 70%. How does Dunn report the $8,000 interest component?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["As part of the installment gain, increasing the gain to $78,000 per payment", "As ordinary interest income, separate from the $70,000 installment gain on the principal payment", "As tax-exempt income because interest on installment notes is not taxable to the seller", "As capital gain because the interest relates to a capital asset sale"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Interest on an installment obligation is reported as ordinary interest income, entirely separate from the installment sale gain computation. The $92,000 principal payment generates $64,400 in gain ($92,000 × 70%), and the $8,000 interest is separately reported as ordinary income. Wrong (A): Interest is not added to the installment gain; they are separate income categories with different character. Wrong (C): Interest received on an installment note is fully taxable as ordinary income. Wrong (D): Interest income retains its ordinary character regardless of the underlying asset's capital gain treatment.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q24: hard / L3 — Section 453B(f) bequest of obligation to the obligor (buyer)
(6, 'Nontaxable Dispositions', $EXPL$Quinn sold rental property to her nephew on the installment method (selling price $500,000, basis $200,000). After receiving $200,000 in payments, Quinn dies and bequeaths the remaining installment obligation (face value $300,000, basis $120,000) to the nephew (the obligor). The obligation is extinguished by the bequest. Under Section 453B(f), what gain does Quinn's estate recognize?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$0 — the stepped-up basis at death eliminates the gain", "$300,000 — the full face value of the cancelled obligation", "$180,000 — the difference between the face value and the basis in the obligation", "$120,000 — the basis in the obligation represents unrecovered cost"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 453B(f), when an installment obligation is cancelled, becomes unenforceable, or is distributed/transmitted to the obligor, it is treated as a disposition. The gain equals the FMV of the obligation (face value of $300,000 for a solvent obligor) minus the basis ($120,000) = $180,000. This is income in respect of a decedent (IRD) reportable by the estate. Wrong (A): Installment obligations are specifically excluded from stepped-up basis treatment under Section 453B(c) and Section 691. Wrong (B): $300,000 is the face value, not the gain; the $120,000 basis must be subtracted. Wrong (D): $120,000 is the basis, not the gain; gain is the excess of FMV over basis.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q25: easy / L1 — Section 1033(a)(2)(A) — controlling stock as replacement property in condemnation
(6, 'Nontaxable Dispositions', $EXPL$Under Section 1033(a)(2)(A), what alternative to purchasing similar-use property is available for deferring gain from an involuntary conversion?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Purchasing any publicly traded stock equal in value to the conversion proceeds", "Depositing the proceeds in a tax-deferred savings account", "Purchasing stock representing 80% or more control of a corporation owning similar-use property", "Investing the proceeds in U.S. Treasury bonds within the replacement period"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 1033(a)(2)(A) allows the taxpayer to defer gain by purchasing controlling stock (80% or more of total combined voting power and value) in a corporation that owns property similar or related in service or use to the converted property. This provides an alternative to direct property replacement. Wrong (A): Purchasing publicly traded stock generally does not qualify; the 80% control requirement must be met for a corporation holding similar-use property. Wrong (B): There is no tax-deferred savings account mechanism under Section 1033. Wrong (D): U.S. Treasury bonds are not similar-use replacement property under Section 1033.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q26: medium / L2 — Section 453 — no installment method for losses
(6, 'Nontaxable Dispositions', $EXPL$Pham sells investment land (adjusted basis $500,000) for $350,000 on a 5-year installment plan ($70,000/year). Pham realizes a $150,000 loss on the sale. Can Pham use the installment method to spread the loss over the 5 years?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Yes, the loss is spread proportionally over the installment payments", "Yes, but only if Pham files Form 6252 for each year", "No, the installment method under Section 453 applies only to gains; Pham must recognize the entire deductible loss in the year of sale", "No, losses on installment sales are permanently disallowed"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 453 applies only to gains from installment sales. When a sale produces a loss, the taxpayer must recognize the full deductible loss in the year of sale under general tax principles. The installment method does not apply to defer or spread losses. Wrong (A): Losses are not spread over installment periods; they are recognized entirely in the year of sale. Wrong (B): Form 6252 is for reporting installment sale income (gains), not losses. Wrong (D): Losses are not permanently disallowed; they are fully deductible in the year of sale (subject to applicable loss limitation rules like Section 1211).$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q27: medium / L3 — Section 1033 — gain character preserved on partial recognition
(6, 'Nontaxable Dispositions', $EXPL$Gibson's rental warehouse (adjusted basis $200,000, original cost $350,000, accumulated straight-line depreciation $150,000) is destroyed by a tornado. Insurance pays $400,000. Gibson purchases replacement property for $370,000. Under Section 1033, Gibson must recognize $30,000 of gain ($400,000 − $370,000). What is the character of the $30,000 recognized gain?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$30,000 long-term capital gain because the warehouse was held over one year", "$30,000 ordinary income under Section 1250 recapture", "$30,000 unrecaptured Section 1250 gain taxed at a maximum 25% rate because straight-line depreciation was used on real property", "$30,000 of Section 1245 ordinary income because depreciation exceeded straight-line"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): The warehouse is real property with $150,000 of straight-line depreciation. Section 1250 recapture for straight-line depreciation on real property produces unrecaptured Section 1250 gain (taxed at max 25%), not ordinary income. The $30,000 recognized gain is characterized as unrecaptured Section 1250 gain to the extent of depreciation, and since $30,000 < $150,000, all $30,000 is unrecaptured Section 1250 gain. Wrong (A): The gain is not simply long-term capital gain; the depreciation creates the 25% unrecaptured Section 1250 layer. Wrong (B): Section 1250 produces ordinary income only for excess depreciation over straight-line; straight-line depreciation produces unrecaptured Section 1250 gain at 25%. Wrong (D): Section 1245 applies to personal property and certain real property types, not a rental warehouse depreciated on a straight-line basis.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q28: medium / L2 — Section 453(e)(1) amount of acceleration capped at gain
(6, 'Nontaxable Dispositions', $EXPL$Marsh sells vacant land (basis $300,000) to her sister for $400,000 on a 10-year installment note in Year 1, receiving only $40,000 in Year 1. The sister resells the land for $390,000 to an unrelated buyer in Year 2 (within the 2-year window). Under Section 453(e), what amount is treated as a payment to Marsh in Year 2?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$400,000 — the full original installment selling price without reduction", "$390,000 — the sister's total resale price without any adjustments", "$360,000 — the face value of the remaining installment obligation balance", "$350,000 — the lesser of remaining obligation or resale price, reduced by prior payments"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Section 453(e)(1), the amount treated as received by the original seller equals the lesser of (i) the amount realized on the second disposition ($390,000) or (ii) the contract price of the first disposition ($400,000), minus payments already received ($40,000). Lesser of $390,000 or $400,000 = $390,000; minus $40,000 = $350,000 treated as a deemed payment. Marsh applies her 25% GP% to $350,000 for gain recognition. Wrong (A): The full contract price is not used directly; it is compared to the second disposition amount and reduced by payments received. Wrong (B): The sister's resale price must be compared to the first disposition contract price, and prior payments are subtracted. Wrong (C): $360,000 is the remaining face value but does not account for the formula comparing both disposition amounts.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — Section 1033 — replacement must be purchased, not gifted or inherited
(6, 'Nontaxable Dispositions', $EXPL$To qualify for gain deferral under Section 1033(a)(2), how must the taxpayer acquire the replacement property?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["By purchase — the taxpayer must acquire the replacement property through a purchase transaction", "By any means, including gift, inheritance, or purchase", "By exchange only — the replacement property must be received directly from the government", "By constructing the replacement property personally; purchased property does not qualify"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Section 1033(a)(2) requires the taxpayer to "purchase" replacement property. Property received by gift, inheritance, or in a nonrecognition exchange generally does not satisfy the purchase requirement. The purchase must use the conversion proceeds or equivalent funds. Wrong (B): Not any acquisition method qualifies; the statute specifically requires a purchase. Wrong (C): An exchange with the government describes Section 1033(a)(1) direct conversions, not the money-reinvestment rule under Section 1033(a)(2). Wrong (D): Purchased property does qualify; in fact, purchase is the required method. Self-construction also qualifies as it involves purchasing materials and labor.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: hard / L4 — Evaluate Section 453 vs Section 1031 integration for deferred exchange with installment boot
(6, 'Nontaxable Dispositions', $EXPL$Delgado completes a Section 1031 deferred exchange, surrendering an office building (FMV $1,200,000, adjusted basis $500,000) and receiving a like-kind warehouse (FMV $950,000) plus $250,000 cash. Delgado's qualified intermediary holds the $250,000 for 90 days before disbursing it. A CPA evaluating the transaction must determine the total recognized gain and whether the $250,000 cash boot qualifies for installment reporting. Assess the correct treatment.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$250,000 of gain is recognized immediately; no installment method is available because the exchange was completed within one tax year", "No gain is recognized because the qualified intermediary held the cash, creating constructive non-receipt", "$250,000 of gain is recognized; installment treatment may apply under Section 453(f)(6) only if the boot is payable in a future tax year per the exchange agreement", "The entire $700,000 gain is recognized because using a qualified intermediary disqualifies the like-kind exchange"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): In a Section 1031 exchange, boot received triggers gain recognition up to the lesser of boot or realized gain. The $250,000 cash boot triggers $250,000 of recognized gain (less than the $700,000 realized gain). Under Section 453(f)(6)(C), installment reporting of boot is available only if the boot obligation extends into a future tax year. If the cash was received in the same tax year as the exchange, installment treatment provides no benefit. The CPA must evaluate whether the payment terms create a qualifying installment obligation. Wrong (A): Installment treatment may be available if the boot terms qualify, but same-year receipt eliminates the benefit. Wrong (B): The qualified intermediary facilitates the exchange but does not eliminate boot recognition when cash is ultimately disbursed to the taxpayer. Wrong (D): A properly structured deferred exchange using a qualified intermediary under Treasury Regulation Section 1.1031(k)-1 is valid.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4)
;

COMMIT;
